<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad Ahmad</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1844–1885</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Muhammad Ahmad al-Mahdi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad Ahmad was a Sudanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> teacher who in 1881 declared himself the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a> and led a revolt against Turco-Egyptian rule in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>. His followers destroyed Egyptian armies and in January 1885 took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a>, killing Charles Gordon. He died months later, and his successor, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdallahi-ibn-muhammad">Khalifa Abdallahi</a>, ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdist-state">Mahdist State</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omdurman">Omdurman</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-ahmad.jpg" alt="Muhammad Ahmad" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Not identified · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Muhammad_Ahmad.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
